1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,300
 - Hello and welcome to Chapter 10.

2
00:00:02,300 --> 00:00:04,220
 Now we're gonna talk about our third kind of collection

3
00:00:04,220 --> 00:00:07,140
 called tuples, but tuples are really a lot like lists.

4
00:00:07,140 --> 00:00:09,440
 There's not too much to them.

5
00:00:09,440 --> 00:00:13,060
 They're really kind of reductionist version of lists there.

6
00:00:13,060 --> 00:00:15,340
 So they function very much like lists,

7
00:00:15,340 --> 00:00:19,500
 in that, you know, they have things,

8
00:00:19,500 --> 00:00:21,540
 and the difference is, is there are no square braces.

9
00:00:21,540 --> 00:00:25,100
 There is a parenthesis, round brace, or whatever,

10
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:27,540
 and they have positions zero, one, and two,

11
00:00:27,540 --> 00:00:31,140
 just like a list, and you can look things up, X sub two.

12
00:00:31,140 --> 00:00:34,260
 So X sub two is actually the third element here,

13
00:00:34,260 --> 00:00:36,500
 and so that prints out Joseph.

14
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:39,100
 You can assign, you know, make a tuple here.

15
00:00:39,100 --> 00:00:41,500
 This is the constant syntax for a tuple,

16
00:00:41,500 --> 00:00:43,220
 and print that out, and the print statement

17
00:00:43,220 --> 00:00:45,420
 shows you that this is a tuple, not a list,

18
00:00:45,420 --> 00:00:46,980
 by showing you round parentheses,

19
00:00:46,980 --> 00:00:49,460
 and a whole bunch of functions that work with lists

20
00:00:49,460 --> 00:00:51,740
 work the same way with tuples.

21
00:00:51,740 --> 00:00:54,500
 You can put a tuple at the end of an end statement

22
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:56,020
 in a four, as you might expect,

23
00:00:56,020 --> 00:00:57,620
 and then it iterates through the tuples.

24
00:00:57,620 --> 00:01:00,820
 Tuples maintain order, so it prints out one, nine, and two.

25
00:01:00,820 --> 00:01:05,300
 So, literally, this bit of code here could be identical,

26
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:07,500
 whether it was a list or a tuple.

27
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:10,260
 It really would do the exact same thing.

28
00:01:10,260 --> 00:01:13,140
 The difference between tuples are that they are immutable.

29
00:01:13,140 --> 00:01:15,580
 Once you create the tuple, you can only,

30
00:01:15,580 --> 00:01:17,940
 sort of, assign a tuple, but you can't modify it.

31
00:01:17,940 --> 00:01:21,540
 You can modify a list, so if we take a look at a list here,

32
00:01:21,540 --> 00:01:23,620
 we make a list that's nine, eight, seven,

33
00:01:23,620 --> 00:01:26,060
 and we say x of two equals six, well,

34
00:01:26,060 --> 00:01:28,220
 that just means this seven becomes a six,

35
00:01:28,220 --> 00:01:32,420
 and that's just natural, meaning we can reassign slots.

36
00:01:32,420 --> 00:01:34,660
 We can delete things, we can insert things,

37
00:01:34,660 --> 00:01:37,500
 we can mutate them, we can change them,

38
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:40,900
 so they're changeable, right, they're changeable.

39
00:01:40,900 --> 00:01:44,700
 But, if we try to do that same thing with a string,

40
00:01:44,700 --> 00:01:46,060
 so we say y equals a, b, c,

41
00:01:46,060 --> 00:01:48,100
 and we know that this is positions zero, one, and two,

42
00:01:48,100 --> 00:01:51,260
 but if we try to say, let's change the c to a d,

43
00:01:51,260 --> 00:01:54,820
 by saying y sub two equals d, that is not allowed,

44
00:01:54,820 --> 00:01:57,180
 and it says it doesn't support item assignment,

45
00:01:57,180 --> 00:02:00,780
 and this little bracket, you know, x sub two,

46
00:02:00,780 --> 00:02:04,260
 is what they call item assignment inside of Python.

47
00:02:04,260 --> 00:02:06,060
 And so, if we do the same thing, then,

48
00:02:06,060 --> 00:02:10,260
 with a three element tuple, put that in z,

49
00:02:10,260 --> 00:02:13,340
 and we try to change this slot to be a zero,

50
00:02:13,340 --> 00:02:15,780
 it's gonna blow up, because it's the exact same thing,

51
00:02:15,780 --> 00:02:17,380
 and that has to do with the fact that,

52
00:02:17,380 --> 00:02:21,220
 once this assignment is made, this is not modifiable.

53
00:02:21,220 --> 00:02:22,780
 Now, it turns out that the reason

54
00:02:22,780 --> 00:02:25,580
 it's not modifiable is for efficiency.

55
00:02:25,580 --> 00:02:31,980
 They take up less storage, they are quicker to access,

56
00:02:31,980 --> 00:02:34,940
 and they're really designed internally, behind the scenes,

57
00:02:34,940 --> 00:02:37,500
 in ways we don't really need to understand.

58
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:40,620
 They're just more efficient than lists,

59
00:02:40,620 --> 00:02:42,060
 if all you wanna do is store a list,

60
00:02:42,060 --> 00:02:43,580
 and look at it, and then throw it away,

61
00:02:43,580 --> 00:02:45,620
 you probably should use a tuple instead.

62
00:02:45,620 --> 00:02:47,820
 So, there's a lot of things that you can do with lists,

63
00:02:47,820 --> 00:02:49,180
 that you also can't do with tuples,

64
00:02:49,180 --> 00:02:51,180
 but they're really just a corollary

65
00:02:51,180 --> 00:02:53,700
 of this notion of non-mutability.

66
00:02:53,700 --> 00:02:55,340
 And so, like, you can sort a list,

67
00:02:55,340 --> 00:02:56,720
 but you can't sort tuples.

68
00:02:56,720 --> 00:02:59,620
 You can add a five to the end of three, two, one,

69
00:02:59,620 --> 00:03:02,100
 can't do that in a tuple, but you can in a list,

70
00:03:02,100 --> 00:03:04,780
 and flip the order, dot, dot, dot, dot, dot, dot.

71
00:03:04,780 --> 00:03:07,260
 So, anything that you can do to a list

72
00:03:07,260 --> 00:03:11,340
 that modifies the list, not allowed for tuples.

73
00:03:11,340 --> 00:03:14,260
 And so, you can take a look at the kinds of things

74
00:03:14,260 --> 00:03:18,820
 that are inside, the methods that are part of each list,

75
00:03:18,820 --> 00:03:21,420
 append, count, extend, index, insert, pop,

76
00:03:21,420 --> 00:03:23,700
 some of these, many of these are modifying,

77
00:03:23,700 --> 00:03:25,340
 and then count and index are the only ones

78
00:03:25,340 --> 00:03:27,740
 that work for tuples.

79
00:03:27,740 --> 00:03:31,220
 And so, tuples are limited lists.

80
00:03:31,220 --> 00:03:33,020
 Now, at some point, there's gonna be a but here

81
00:03:33,020 --> 00:03:35,260
 to say, "Why do we like them?"

82
00:03:35,260 --> 00:03:37,680
 And the reason that we like them

83
00:03:37,680 --> 00:03:39,180
 is that they're just more efficient.

84
00:03:39,180 --> 00:03:41,700
 They don't have to build Python

85
00:03:41,700 --> 00:03:44,980
 in its own internal organization of these objects.

86
00:03:44,980 --> 00:03:47,660
 It knows that they'll never be modified,

87
00:03:47,660 --> 00:03:48,680
 'cause when you make a tuple,

88
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:49,820
 you as the programmer are saying,

89
00:03:49,820 --> 00:03:51,540
 "I'm never gonna modify this,"

90
00:03:51,540 --> 00:03:53,820
 and Python won't let you do it.

91
00:03:53,820 --> 00:03:56,500
 So, it's higher performance, better memory use,

92
00:03:56,500 --> 00:03:59,340
 and to a beginning programmer, that doesn't really matter,

93
00:03:59,340 --> 00:04:00,860
 but that's the reason.

94
00:04:00,860 --> 00:04:04,460
 And so, we tend to use tuples in situations

95
00:04:04,460 --> 00:04:05,760
 where we're gonna make a temporary variable

96
00:04:05,760 --> 00:04:08,340
 and then temporarily use it just a little bit

97
00:04:08,340 --> 00:04:10,220
 and then throw it away without really messing with it.

98
00:04:10,220 --> 00:04:12,460
 And we tend to use lists to build things up,

99
00:04:12,460 --> 00:04:14,060
 et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

100
00:04:15,820 --> 00:04:18,960
 So, the other thing that's interesting about tuples,

101
00:04:18,960 --> 00:04:20,620
 and we've actually sort of seen this,

102
00:04:20,620 --> 00:04:24,380
 is that you can put a tuple that includes variables

103
00:04:24,380 --> 00:04:26,280
 on the left side of the assignment.

104
00:04:26,280 --> 00:04:29,060
 And this takes a little getting used to,

105
00:04:29,060 --> 00:04:30,580
 but it's really cool.

106
00:04:30,580 --> 00:04:32,780
 And no other language that I know of does this.

107
00:04:32,780 --> 00:04:35,700
 So, if we say x comma y, that's a two-tuple.

108
00:04:35,700 --> 00:04:36,760
 Both have to be variables.

109
00:04:36,760 --> 00:04:38,740
 You can't put constants on this side.

110
00:04:38,740 --> 00:04:42,740
 You know, it's like saying x equals four, y equals Fred.

111
00:04:42,740 --> 00:04:44,540
 Right?

112
00:04:44,540 --> 00:04:47,220
 So, what happens is, is you can put a tuple

113
00:04:47,220 --> 00:04:48,940
 on the far side of an assignment statement.

114
00:04:48,940 --> 00:04:52,180
 And the four goes to x and the Fred goes to y.

115
00:04:52,180 --> 00:04:53,140
 And you say, what's in y?

116
00:04:53,140 --> 00:04:54,340
 Well, y is indeed Fred.

117
00:04:54,340 --> 00:04:56,660
 And so, this is like two assignment statements.

118
00:04:56,660 --> 00:04:58,460
 Now, the way I've got this syntax,

119
00:04:58,460 --> 00:05:00,660
 I would probably do two separate statements,

120
00:05:00,660 --> 00:05:03,260
 just not to show off that I know how to do tuples.

121
00:05:03,260 --> 00:05:07,060
 You know, and so, here's another one,

122
00:05:07,060 --> 00:05:08,980
 and they just move correspondingly.

123
00:05:08,980 --> 00:05:11,540
 If you don't have two here and you do have two here,

124
00:05:12,480 --> 00:05:15,660
 well, if you have three here, or two here and three here,

125
00:05:15,660 --> 00:05:17,060
 and you don't match the number there,

126
00:05:17,060 --> 00:05:18,000
 you get in some trouble.

127
00:05:18,000 --> 00:05:20,700
 Now, if you just say x equals tuple,

128
00:05:20,700 --> 00:05:22,420
 then that is the tuple in the list.

129
00:05:22,420 --> 00:05:26,660
 But this is just a simple, straight 99 value going into a.

130
00:05:26,660 --> 00:05:29,960
 So, you can put tuples as the left-hand side.

131
00:05:29,960 --> 00:05:31,040
 And you can even do things like

132
00:05:31,040 --> 00:05:32,900
 return a tuple from functions.

133
00:05:32,900 --> 00:05:36,380
 That's a real nice Python feature that I like a lot.

134
00:05:36,380 --> 00:05:38,380
 Tuples are also related to dictionaries,

135
00:05:38,380 --> 00:05:40,420
 as we've seen in the previous chapter.

136
00:05:40,420 --> 00:05:41,820
 So, here we make a little dictionary.

137
00:05:41,820 --> 00:05:43,140
 We make an empty dictionary

138
00:05:43,140 --> 00:05:44,740
 by constructing an empty dictionary.

139
00:05:44,740 --> 00:05:47,360
 Stick it in D, so D is sort of like this place

140
00:05:47,360 --> 00:05:49,300
 that can hold key-value pairs.

141
00:05:49,300 --> 00:05:52,300
 And we put csev, and there's a two in there,

142
00:05:52,300 --> 00:05:54,400
 and chen1, and there's a four in there.

143
00:05:54,400 --> 00:05:56,420
 So, we have this associative mapping

144
00:05:56,420 --> 00:05:59,020
 between csev and two, and chen1 and four.

145
00:05:59,020 --> 00:06:00,180
 All stuff we know.

146
00:06:00,180 --> 00:06:02,640
 And now, we say, hey, we're gonna loop

147
00:06:02,640 --> 00:06:04,380
 through the key-value pairs here,

148
00:06:04,380 --> 00:06:07,820
 and we've seen this syntax before, k comma v.

149
00:06:07,820 --> 00:06:09,160
 So, this is a tuple.

150
00:06:09,160 --> 00:06:11,340
 So, you can think of this as each one of these things

151
00:06:11,340 --> 00:06:13,340
 is going to get assigned into this tuple,

152
00:06:13,340 --> 00:06:15,020
 which means the key ends up in,

153
00:06:15,020 --> 00:06:17,020
 and the first one's the key, and the second one's the value.

154
00:06:17,020 --> 00:06:20,780
 I use the variable kv all the time in code that I write,

155
00:06:20,780 --> 00:06:22,100
 just for my own sanity.

156
00:06:22,100 --> 00:06:24,540
 So, kv are gonna iterate successively

157
00:06:24,540 --> 00:06:28,140
 through the successive keys and values in that.

158
00:06:28,140 --> 00:06:29,940
 So, this is gonna run twice,

159
00:06:29,940 --> 00:06:32,940
 and k is gonna be csev two, and chen1 four.

160
00:06:32,940 --> 00:06:35,620
 The order just happened to stay the same.

161
00:06:35,620 --> 00:06:40,940
 And so, if you say, what is in one of these things,

162
00:06:40,940 --> 00:06:42,540
 you can actually take d items,

163
00:06:42,540 --> 00:06:44,700
 the items method within that dictionary,

164
00:06:44,700 --> 00:06:48,940
 and say, hey, give that to me back, and then print tups.

165
00:06:48,940 --> 00:06:51,540
 And this is, it's a special kind of a class,

166
00:06:51,540 --> 00:06:54,860
 but really, ultimately, it is a list of tuples.

167
00:06:54,860 --> 00:06:57,380
 You know, this is two, this is the zero,

168
00:06:57,380 --> 00:07:00,220
 and this is the two, the one, the first and the second.

169
00:07:00,220 --> 00:07:03,260
 And then, within each thing you get, you have a two-tuple.

170
00:07:03,260 --> 00:07:07,860
 And so, in a sense, this k and v are iterating

171
00:07:07,860 --> 00:07:11,300
 through those things when we're putting d items here

172
00:07:11,300 --> 00:07:12,700
 and d items there.

173
00:07:12,700 --> 00:07:16,820
 One nice thing about tuples is that they're comparable.

174
00:07:16,820 --> 00:07:18,220
 They're comparable in the same way

175
00:07:18,220 --> 00:07:19,340
 that strings are comparable,

176
00:07:19,340 --> 00:07:21,500
 meaning that they're compared from left to right

177
00:07:21,500 --> 00:07:25,140
 with the leftmost or zero tuple being the most significant.

178
00:07:25,140 --> 00:07:27,700
 And it doesn't compare any further than it has to

179
00:07:27,700 --> 00:07:30,560
 if it's asking less than.

180
00:07:30,560 --> 00:07:32,820
 So, if it's looking at, say, this first tuple,

181
00:07:32,820 --> 00:07:34,140
 it starts at the left and says,

182
00:07:34,140 --> 00:07:37,420
 okay, ask the question, tell me true or false.

183
00:07:37,420 --> 00:07:38,940
 Is zero less than five?

184
00:07:38,940 --> 00:07:40,180
 The answer is true.

185
00:07:40,180 --> 00:07:43,220
 And so, the answer to this overall expression is true,

186
00:07:43,220 --> 00:07:45,860
 and it doesn't even compare those two numbers.

187
00:07:45,860 --> 00:07:49,140
 Those second and third number, they don't compare them.

188
00:07:49,140 --> 00:07:53,000
 If, on the other hand, we're asking is this less than that,

189
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:54,580
 it only looks at the first one

190
00:07:54,580 --> 00:07:56,460
 and asks if it can answer the question.

191
00:07:56,460 --> 00:07:58,500
 And the answer is, well, they're both zero,

192
00:07:58,500 --> 00:08:00,020
 and so I can't answer the question,

193
00:08:00,020 --> 00:08:02,380
 so I have to go to the second one, second pair,

194
00:08:02,380 --> 00:08:05,740
 and one is less than three, and so that means this is true.

195
00:08:05,740 --> 00:08:07,940
 And it does not check this,

196
00:08:07,940 --> 00:08:10,940
 even though 20 million is bigger than four.

197
00:08:10,940 --> 00:08:14,820
 It doesn't matter because these are the numbers

198
00:08:14,820 --> 00:08:17,660
 that caused the true to happen.

199
00:08:17,660 --> 00:08:22,660
 And the same is true if you do this with strings.

200
00:08:22,660 --> 00:08:24,580
 Again, we start the first one.

201
00:08:24,580 --> 00:08:27,380
 So, Jones, Sally, well, that's the same,

202
00:08:27,380 --> 00:08:28,540
 so we don't know the answer yet.

203
00:08:28,540 --> 00:08:30,540
 And so, Sally, Sam, well, okay.

204
00:08:30,540 --> 00:08:33,100
 S, S, oh, they're the same.

205
00:08:33,100 --> 00:08:35,180
 A, A, they're the same.

206
00:08:35,180 --> 00:08:38,020
 O, L, and M.

207
00:08:38,020 --> 00:08:41,820
 L is less than M, so the actual letter

208
00:08:41,820 --> 00:08:44,140
 that makes the difference here, the L and the M,

209
00:08:44,140 --> 00:08:46,220
 and leads to us being true.

210
00:08:46,220 --> 00:08:48,060
 And so it goes left to right,

211
00:08:48,060 --> 00:08:49,500
 but then even when it's doing strings,

212
00:08:49,500 --> 00:08:50,340
 it's going left to right.

213
00:08:50,340 --> 00:08:53,540
 That's just how string comparison works.

214
00:08:53,540 --> 00:08:58,540
 And if we say, is Jones,

215
00:08:58,540 --> 00:09:00,780
 Jones, Sally greater than Adam, Sam?

216
00:09:00,780 --> 00:09:02,020
 Well, we check the first one,

217
00:09:02,020 --> 00:09:03,700
 and we check the J and the A.

218
00:09:03,700 --> 00:09:05,740
 Well, J is greater than A,

219
00:09:05,740 --> 00:09:07,220
 and so we don't have to look at anything else.

220
00:09:07,220 --> 00:09:09,580
 We don't have to look at any more of these characters.

221
00:09:09,580 --> 00:09:11,660
 We don't have to look at the second thing in the tuple.

222
00:09:11,660 --> 00:09:14,140
 We have to look at, well, that is enough to be true.

223
00:09:14,140 --> 00:09:17,580
 So it only scans until it has a definitive answer.

224
00:09:17,580 --> 00:09:19,420
 It doesn't scan any further.

225
00:09:19,420 --> 00:09:21,980
 So now, what we're gonna do

226
00:09:21,980 --> 00:09:24,140
 is use this comparable capability

227
00:09:24,140 --> 00:09:25,540
 to sort these list of tuples,

228
00:09:25,540 --> 00:09:27,420
 and then bring this all back

229
00:09:27,420 --> 00:09:29,220
 and connect it more to dictionaries.

230
00:09:29,220 --> 00:09:32,720
 (piano music)

231
00:09:33,700 --> 00:09:36,200
 (piano music)

